    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d507">

                <pb facs="0605" n="593" xml:id="pg593"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.24/92: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-11-20T12:00:00+02:00">November 20,
                            1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 21—12:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>320. Department’s telegram No. 262, November 15, 8 p.m., Department’s 266,
                    November 16, 3 p.m., and my 316, November 18, 7 p.m. In a written communication
                    dated November 19 the Prime Minister expresses his great appreciation of the
                    President’s prompt response to his appeal for the material aid so necessary to
                    Greece in its hard and unequal struggle against an unprovoked aggression.</p>
                <p>He confirms the full agreement of the Greek Government with the Department’s
                    point of view regarding Greek purchases in the United States stating that orders
                    will be placed by the Greek Minister at Washington through the British
                    Purchasing Commission liaison with which will be assured by the Greek Consul
                    General at New York. He adds that lists of materials required have been drawn up
                    in agreement with the British authorities and transmitted to the Greek Minister
                    and that the necessary instructions have been issued by both the Greek and
                    British Governments.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
